    {
      "id": 4,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"preparation\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research bounded-memory top-k selection, initially retaining the existing parser. First test a full-neighborhood cutoff check that rejects non-improving points before the current linear search. Then replace insertion with a max-heap ordered by (float distance, original encounter ordinal), giving O(N log k) worst-case selection with O(k) workspace. Equal-distance later points must not displace earlier ones. Compare a small-k sorted buffer and a direct k=1 scan where justified by measurements. Replace label qsort with a frequency table that preserves the target's plain-char ordering for vote ties. Preserve underfilled-neighborhood padding semantics. Deliver independently validated candidates and unchanged-harness timings; do not presume the heap wins for every k.\",\"B\":\"Research an independent full-distance selection algorithm for larger neighborhoods. Compute distance records and use robust partial selection rather than sorting all N points. Preserve cutoff ties through a (distance, encounter ordinal) key, or select a distance threshold and resolve its equal-distance boundary in original encounter order. Never depend on partition stability. Use robust pivoting and a fallback to prevent quadratic degeneration; account for the additional O(N) workspace and allocation-size overflow. Establish a serial candidate first. If supported by the unchanged build and remaining budget, investigate ARM64 vectorization across independent points and up to four workers for distance generation, preserving reference floating-point evaluation. Measure whether parallel overhead or memory traffic erases the gain.\",\"C\":\"Research input and storage throughput independently of selection: initially leave knn unchanged. Replace repeated scanf calls with buffered fread-based parsing and verified float conversion, such as strtof, rather than an approximate fixed-decimal parser. Preserve the source grammar, including n_groups=, raw label characters, valid whitespace, signs, exponents and float rounding. Handle tokens crossing buffer boundaries, EOF and non-seekable stdin. Because k and the query arrive after all groups, retain the coordinates; do not assume distances can be computed while reading. Investigate contiguous storage and fewer allocations while preserving group traversal order and compatible interfaces where practical. Report end-to-end gains with the original selector so parsing benefits are distinguishable from algorithmic changes.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only correctness-preserving candidates produced by A/B/C. Keep the baseline and each independent winner available. Within the existing evaluation budget, compare the winning selectors both with and without C's parser/storage changes; measure threading separately where available. Do not assume improvements combine additively. Use only justified runtime-size dispatch between algorithms, never recognized test values, labels or expected outputs. Audit distance rounding, encounter-order cutoff ties, label ordering, padding, allocation arithmetic and NaN fallbacks across interfaces. Select using the unchanged harness's end-to-end measurements and prescribed repetitions on the fixed four-core ARM64 target. Revert regressions and report measured lineage and correctness status. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler/build configuration, budgets, target or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The target is the supplied KNN program; A/B/C are research roles, not the other official problems. With N total points, the baseline performs O(Nk) neighbor maintenance, O(k log k) label sorting and repeated scanf parsing. The only timing evidence is a valid baseline averaging 2.0080 seconds over three samples (2.0066–2.0094), with four correctness cases; there is no size distribution or bottleneck profile. Therefore investigate selection and parsing independently. All candidates must preserve float squared-distance evaluation, earlier encounter order at equal-distance cutoffs, smallest-label vote ties under compare_for_sort's target plain-char ordering, and the single-character output. Match floating-point contraction/rounding rather than introducing approximations or reassociation. NaN distances do not obey the ordering assumed by heap/partition selection: retain a reference-equivalent fallback. Preserve sentinel-label padding for supported k>N inputs. Four passing cases alone do not establish these invariants. This is one query, supplied after the points, so spatial-index construction is not a priority.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"46356809606d6fa8eeabf4f89cf7aab6719400d7991be40bd0e50bfb75e4bc81\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research bounded-memory top-k selection, initially retaining the existing parser. First test a full-neighborhood cutoff check that rejects non-improving points before the current linear search. Then replace insertion with a max-heap ordered by (float distance, original encounter ordinal), giving O(N log k) worst-case selection with O(k) workspace. Equal-distance later points must not displace earlier ones. Compare a small-k sorted buffer and a direct k=1 scan where justified by measurements. Replace label qsort with a frequency table that preserves the target's plain-char ordering for vote ties. Preserve underfilled-neighborhood padding semantics. Deliver independently validated candidates and unchanged-harness timings; do not presume the heap wins for every k.\",\"B\":\"Research an independent full-distance selection algorithm for larger neighborhoods. Compute distance records and use robust partial selection rather than sorting all N points. Preserve cutoff ties through a (distance, encounter ordinal) key, or select a distance threshold and resolve its equal-distance boundary in original encounter order. Never depend on partition stability. Use robust pivoting and a fallback to prevent quadratic degeneration; account for the additional O(N) workspace and allocation-size overflow. Establish a serial candidate first. If supported by the unchanged build and remaining budget, investigate ARM64 vectorization across independent points and up to four workers for distance generation, preserving reference floating-point evaluation. Measure whether parallel overhead or memory traffic erases the gain.\",\"C\":\"Research input and storage throughput independently of selection: initially leave knn unchanged. Replace repeated scanf calls with buffered fread-based parsing and verified float conversion, such as strtof, rather than an approximate fixed-decimal parser. Preserve the source grammar, including n_groups=, raw label characters, valid whitespace, signs, exponents and float rounding. Handle tokens crossing buffer boundaries, EOF and non-seekable stdin. Because k and the query arrive after all groups, retain the coordinates; do not assume distances can be computed while reading. Investigate contiguous storage and fewer allocations while preserving group traversal order and compatible interfaces where practical. Report end-to-end gains with the original selector so parsing benefits are distinguishable from algorithmic changes.\",\"G\":\"Integrate only correctness-preserving candidates produced by A/B/C. Keep the baseline and each independent winner available. Within the existing evaluation budget, compare the winning selectors both with and without C's parser/storage changes; measure threading separately where available. Do not assume improvements combine additively. Use only justified runtime-size dispatch between algorithms, never recognized test values, labels or expected outputs. Audit distance rounding, encounter-order cutoff ties, label ordering, padding, allocation arithmetic and NaN fallbacks across interfaces. Select using the unchanged harness's end-to-end measurements and prescribed repetitions on the fixed four-core ARM64 target. Revert regressions and report measured lineage and correctness status. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler/build configuration, budgets, target or permissions.\",\"analysis\":\"The target is the supplied KNN program; A/B/C are research roles, not the other official problems. With N total points, the baseline performs O(Nk) neighbor maintenance, O(k log k) label sorting and repeated scanf parsing. The only timing evidence is a valid baseline averaging 2.0080 seconds over three samples (2.0066–2.0094), with four correctness cases; there is no size distribution or bottleneck profile. Therefore investigate selection and parsing independently. All candidates must preserve float squared-distance evaluation, earlier encounter order at equal-distance cutoffs, smallest-label vote ties under compare_for_sort's target plain-char ordering, and the single-character output. Match floating-point contraction/rounding rather than introducing approximations or reassociation. NaN distances do not obey the ordering assumed by heap/partition selection: retain a reference-equivalent fallback. Preserve sentinel-label padding for supported k>N inputs. Four passing cases alone do not establish these invariants. This is one query, supplied after the points, so spatial-index construction is not a priority.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-astra\",\"nonce\":\"2b02aa5984f24fe5a916aa95b8ce2aa2\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"46356809606d6fa8eeabf4f89cf7aab6719400d7991be40bd0e50bfb75e4bc81\",\"session_id\":\"aa970cf8bf5d4c1aaa0fdabb21d73a72\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"f45cb23b9fd4f8ad583a214776da0ff5b33f6a18d9703edc8f3717a704e77992\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":10907,\"output_tokens\":6299,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":5468}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-astra\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":17206,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"comp